@*
	The unread-mail dot: a small red circle carrying a number, sat on the thing it counts.

	Its own component because it is drawn for one number in more than one place - the info
	page's Join requests button, the live map's hamburger, and that menu's own Join requests
	item - and a circle whose size and colour were typed out three times is a circle that ends
	up three slightly different sizes.

	It draws the pill and nothing else. **Where it sits is the caller's business**: in a line of
	text it is an inline item, on the corner of a 48 px map control it is pinned and hanging
	over the edge, and those are facts about those two screens rather than about a badge. The
	one that hangs on a corner also has to restate the colours, because it is over a map - see
	GroupRideLive's `.chrome > button.hamburger .count-badge`.

	Absent at zero rather than drawn as "0". A badge is a claim that something is waiting; one
	that says nothing is waiting is a permanent decoration a reader learns to stop seeing.

	The number is read out as words, not as a bare digit. A screen reader meeting "3" beside
	"Join requests" has no way to say whether that is a count, a position or a shortcut key,
	so the visible glyph is hidden from it and Label is what it gets instead.
*@

@if (Count > 0)
{
	<span class="count-badge" role="status" aria-label="@Label">
		<span aria-hidden="true">@Text</span>
	</span>
}

@code {
	/// <summary>How many are waiting. Nothing is drawn at zero or below.</summary>
	[Parameter] public int Count { get; set; }

	/// <summary>What the number is, for a screen reader: "join requests", "unread messages".</summary>
	[Parameter] public string Noun { get; set; } = "waiting";

	/// <summary>
	/// Past 99 the badge stops counting and starts saying "a lot". Three digits inside a circle
	/// this size is unreadable at arm's length, and the difference between 100 and 140 waiting
	/// changes nothing about what the reader does next.
	/// </summary>
	private string Text => Count > 99 ? "99+" : Count.ToString();

	private string Label => $"{Count} {Noun}";
}
